    title: "Greetings and respect: po and opo",
    blurb: "Greet anyone at any time of day, say thank you and goodbye, and show respect with po, opo and kayo.",
    deck: "Greetings and respect",
    body: `
<p>Filipinos greet by the time of day, and they show respect in almost every sentence they say to an older person. Both are easy once you see the pattern.</p>
<h3>Good morning, good afternoon, good evening</h3>
<p>English says "good morning". Tagalog says "beautiful morning": <b>maganda</b> (beautiful, fine) joined to the time of day with the linker <b>-ng</b>. That is why every greeting starts with <b>Magandang</b>.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>When</th><th>Greeting</th></tr><tr><td>morning</td><td class="k">Magandang umaga</td></tr><tr><td>around noon</td><td class="k">Magandang tanghali</td></tr><tr><td>afternoon</td><td class="k">Magandang hapon</td></tr><tr><td>evening and night</td><td class="k">Magandang gabi</td></tr></table></div>
{{ex:Magandang umaga po.|mah-gahn-DAHNG oo-MAH-gah pohq|Good morning. (polite)}}
{{ex:Magandang hapon.|mah-gahn-DAHNG HAH-pohn|Good afternoon.}}
<h3>po and opo: respect in one small word</h3>
<p>English shows respect with "sir" or "ma'am" now and then. Tagalog adds <b>po</b> to the sentence, right after the first word or phrase. Use it with anyone older than you, adult strangers, customers and staff you do not know, your parents and grandparents. Leave it out with friends, children and people younger than you. To say yes politely, say <b>opo</b>; <b>oo</b> is the plain yes for friends.</p>
{{ex:Salamat po.|sah-LAH-maht pohq|Thank you. (polite)}}
{{ex:Opo, Lola.|OH-pohq LOH-lah|Yes, Grandma.}}
<p>You will also hear <b>ho</b> and <b>oho</b>; they mean the same as <b>po</b> and <b>opo</b>.</p>
<h3>How are you?</h3>
<p>To a friend: <b>Kumusta ka?</b> To an older person, Tagalog uses the plural "you", <b>kayo</b>, even for one person, together with <b>po</b>. The usual answer is <b>Mabuti naman</b> (I'm fine).</p>
{{ex:Kumusta ka?|koo-moos-TAH kah|How are you? (to a friend)}}
{{ex:Kumusta po kayo?|koo-moos-TAH poh kah-YOH|How are you? (polite)}}
{{ex:Mabuti naman po.|mah-BOO-tee nah-MAHN pohq|I'm fine. (polite)}}
<h3>Thanks and goodbye</h3>
<p><b>Salamat</b> is thank you, and the reply is <b>Walang anuman</b> (you're welcome). When someone leaves, Filipinos say <b>Ingat!</b> (take care) more often than <b>Paalam</b> (goodbye), which sounds formal.</p>
{{ex:Walang anuman.|wah-LAHNG ah-noo-MAHN|You're welcome.}}
{{ex:Ingat po.|EE-ngaht pohq|Take care. (polite)}}
<h3>Titles: kuya, ate, tito, tita</h3>
<p>A young man a bit older than you, including a driver or a guard, is <b>Kuya</b> (big brother); a young woman is <b>Ate</b> (big sister, said AH-teh). Your parents' friends are <b>Tito</b> and <b>Tita</b>. In shops you will also hear <b>sir</b> and <b>ma'am</b>.</p>
{{ex:Salamat po, Ate.|sah-LAH-maht pohq AH-teh|Thank you, miss.}}
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> answering an elder with the plain yes. <b>Oo, Lola.</b> → <b>Opo, Lola.</b> And to an elder, <b>Kumusta ka po?</b> → <b>Kumusta po kayo?</b></div>
`,
