    id: "pronouns",
    title: "ang pronouns: ako, ikaw / ka, siya, kami, tayo, kayo, sila",
    blurb: "Say I, you, he, she, we and they in Tagalog, choose the right we, and put si before a name.",
    deck: "Family and people",
    body: `
<p>These are the ang pronouns, the forms for the person a sentence is about. Later you will meet two more sets (<b>ko</b>, <b>akin</b>…); for now, these seven do the work of English "I, you, he, she, we, they".</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>English</th><th>Tagalog</th></tr><tr><td>I</td><td class="k">ako</td></tr><tr><td>you (one friend)</td><td class="k">ikaw / ka</td></tr><tr><td>he, she</td><td class="k">siya</td></tr><tr><td>we (not you)</td><td class="k">kami</td></tr><tr><td>we (with you)</td><td class="k">tayo</td></tr><tr><td>you (several people, or one person politely)</td><td class="k">kayo</td></tr><tr><td>they</td><td class="k">sila</td></tr></table></div>
<h3>si before a name</h3>
<p>English says "I am Ana". Tagalog has no word for "am" here; it puts the little word <b>si</b> before the name: <b>Ako si Ana</b>. Every person's name gets <b>si</b> in this spot, never <b>ang</b>.</p>
{{ex:Ako si Ana.|ah-KOH see AH-nah|I'm Ana.}}
{{ex:Siya si Juan.|shah see hwahn|He's Juan.}}
<h3>One word for he and she</h3>
<p><b>siya</b> means both "he" and "she" (it is not used for things). The name or the situation tells you which.</p>
<h3>ikaw or ka</h3>
<p>Both mean "you" for one friend. <b>ikaw</b> starts a sentence; <b>ka</b> never does, it comes right after the first word. The meaning is the same.</p>
{{ex:Ikaw si Mark.|ee-KOW see mahrk|You're Mark.}}
{{ex:Si Mark ka.|see mahrk kah|You're Mark.}}
<p>Tagalog often puts the new information first and the pronoun last, the reverse of English: <b>Guro ako</b> is "I'm a teacher", word for word "teacher I".</p>
{{ex:Guro ako.|GOO-roh ah-KOH|I'm a teacher.}}
<h3>Two words for we</h3>
<p>English "we" can mean "me and others, not you" or "you and me". Tagalog keeps them apart. <b>kami</b> leaves the listener out; <b>tayo</b> includes them. Talking about your own family to a Filipino friend, say <b>kami</b>. Inviting everyone at the table to eat, say <b>tayo</b>.</p>
{{ex:Guro kami.|GOO-roh kah-MEE|We're teachers. (not you)}}
{{ex:Kain tayo!|KAH-een TAH-yoh|Let's eat!}}
<h3>kayo: more than one, or polite</h3>
<p><b>kayo</b> is "you" for a group, and also the respectful "you" for one older person, with <b>po</b>, as in <b>Kumusta po kayo?</b></p>
{{ex:Mga guro sila.|mah-NGAH GOO-roh see-LAH|They're teachers.}}
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> putting <b>ang</b> before a name. <b>Ako ang Ana.</b> → <b>Ako si Ana.</b> And <b>ka</b> never starts a sentence: <b>Ka si Mark.</b> → <b>Ikaw si Mark.</b></div>
`,
