    id: "intro",
    title: "Introducing yourself: Ako si…, Taga-… ako",
    blurb: "Introduce yourself: your name, where you are from, your age, and that you are learning Tagalog.",
    deck: "About me",
    body: `
<p>When you meet Filipinos, the first questions are almost always the same: your name, where you are from, and often your age. Here are the questions and the answers.</p>
<h3>Your name</h3>
<p>You already know <b>Ako si…</b> with <b>si</b> before the name. To ask a friend's name, say <b>Ano ang pangalan mo?</b> (word for word "What the name your?"). In speech you will usually hear <b>Anong pangalan mo?</b>, with <b>ano</b> joined to the noun by the linker <b>-ng</b> (some people write <b>ano'ng</b>, short for <b>ano ang</b>). To an older person: <b>Ano po ang pangalan ninyo?</b></p>
{{ex:Ano ang pangalan mo?|ah-NOH ahng pah-NGAH-lahn moh|What's your name? (to a friend)}}
{{ex:Anong pangalan mo?|ah-NOHNG pah-NGAH-lahn moh|What's your name? (to a friend)}}
{{ex:Ako si Carlo.|ah-KOH see KAHR-loh|I'm Carlo.}}
<h3>Where you are from: taga-</h3>
<p>English says "I'm from Manila". Tagalog puts <b>taga-</b> (from) in front of the place, with a hyphen, and the pronoun at the end: <b>Taga-Maynila ako</b>. To ask, put <b>taga-</b> on <b>saan</b> (where). Answer with your city, your state or your country; a Filipino relative may also ask which province your family comes from.</p>
{{ex:Taga-saan ka?|tah-gah-sah-AHN kah|Where are you from? (to a friend)}}
{{ex:Taga-saan po kayo?|tah-gah-sah-AHN poh kah-YOH|Where are you from? (polite)}}
{{ex:Taga-Amerika ako.|tah-gah-ah-MEH-ree-kah ah-KOH|I'm from the US.}}
<h3>Nationality</h3>
<p>For nationality, the word itself comes first: <b>Amerikano ako</b> (I'm American), <b>Pilipino ako</b> (I'm Filipino). Many heritage learners say <b>Pilipino-Amerikano ako</b>.</p>
{{ex:Amerikano ako.|ah-meh-ree-KAH-noh ah-KOH|I'm American.}}
<h3>Learning Tagalog</h3>
<p>Learn this sentence as one block for now; the verb will make sense in the verb lessons. People will be delighted to hear it, and many will switch to slow, careful Tagalog for you.</p>
{{ex:Nag-aaral ako ng Tagalog.|nahg-ah-AH-rahl ah-KOH nahng tah-GAH-lohg|I'm studying Tagalog.}}
<h3>Your age</h3>
<p>Filipinos ask age openly; it decides who gets <b>po</b>, <b>kuya</b> or <b>ate</b>. English asks "How old?"; Tagalog asks "How many years already?": <b>ilan</b> (how many) + the linker <b>-g</b> + <b>taon</b> (year). After a word ending in <b>n</b>, the linker is just <b>-g</b>.</p>
{{ex:Ilang taon ka na?|ee-LAHNG tah-OHN kah nah|How old are you? (to a friend)}}
{{ex:Dalawampu't limang taon na ako.|dah-lah-wahm-POOT lee-MAHNG tah-OHN nah ah-KOH|I'm twenty-five.}}
<p>The numbers come in a later lesson; until then, you can answer with the English number, which everyone understands.</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> translating "How are you?" word for word. <b>Paano ka?</b> → <b>Kumusta ka?</b> And do not drop <b>taga-</b>: <b>Ako ay Amerika.</b> → <b>Taga-Amerika ako.</b></div>
`,
